1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:06,035
This is Halflife — a use-by date for cryptographic security, on Solana.

2
00:00:06,555 --> 00:00:10,714
Your program stops trusting a circuit the moment its evidence expires.

3
00:00:11,433 --> 00:00:16,682
In 2026, four cryptographic systems that had passed professional review failed.

4
00:00:16,702 --> 00:00:22,149
Three had been hiding for four to six years. At least two were found by AI-assisted audits.

5
00:00:22,767 --> 00:00:30,599
Zcash Orchard shipped in 2022, after review. Four years later, an AI-assisted audit found a counterfeiting bug in it.

6
00:00:30,619 --> 00:00:33,377
The code never changed. The tools got better.

7
00:00:33,697 --> 00:00:40,406
So an audit is a perishable good. And a broken zero-knowledge circuit fails silently, with valid-looking proofs.

8
00:00:40,933 --> 00:00:49,556
Halflife gives a circuit a passport: a signed, 125-byte claim of what was verified, on which build — and when it expires.

9
00:00:49,576 --> 00:00:55,691
Solana enforces it. Hyperlane carries it across chains. Programs refuse to proceed without one.

10
00:00:56,400 --> 00:01:02,631
This is the live control room. Every number on it comes from real exercises on Solana devnet.

11
00:01:02,951 --> 00:01:07,881
Even the Hyperlane message is real, dispatched through the deployed devnet mailbox.

12
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:14,343
The halo2_gadgets advisory lists its affected crates, then says: “and any dependents thereof.”

13
00:01:14,663 --> 00:01:16,278
Nothing enumerates them.

14
00:01:17,198 --> 00:01:18,065
This does.

15
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:24,173
The patched version still reaches three circuits — and affects none of them.

16
00:01:24,493 --> 00:01:30,853
Anyone can build an alarm. Showing what a healthy dependency doesn't touch is what proves it discriminates.

17
00:01:31,467 --> 00:01:38,495
You can run the same check on your own project. Paste a Cargo.lock and your browser asks OSV directly.

18
00:01:39,115 --> 00:01:41,670
The vulnerable Orchard lockfile: WOULD FAIL.

19
00:01:42,690 --> 00:01:44,210
The patched one: WOULD PASS.

20
00:01:44,933 --> 00:01:51,097
And it runs in CI: one line of YAML fails a pull request that pulls in a vulnerable dependency.

21
00:01:51,833 --> 00:01:58,158
Now, enforcement. A fire drill recorded on Solana devnet — every signature is on the explorer.

22
00:01:58,478 --> 00:02:04,518
A finding lands, the passport is invalidated, and a third-party program refuses to proceed.

23
00:02:04,838 --> 00:02:11,621
That check costs 1,520 compute units — and read-only passports let Sealevel run checks in parallel.

24
00:02:12,233 --> 00:02:13,561
Now the one that matters.

25
00:02:13,981 --> 00:02:19,102
A passport is published — and then nothing. No invalidation. The relayer simply stops.

26
00:02:21,322 --> 00:02:25,679
41 seconds later, the consumer blocks anyway — on expiry alone.

27
00:02:26,199 --> 00:02:30,458
A passport can't vouch for its own freshness. The reader checks its own clock.

28
00:02:30,678 --> 00:02:33,556
So withholding a message produces the safe outcome.

29
00:02:34,567 --> 00:02:38,068
Don't want to trust our dashboard? Press Read from devnet.

30
00:02:38,488 --> 00:02:44,425
Your browser pulls the real passport from Solana and decodes the 125 bytes itself.

31
00:02:45,133 --> 00:02:49,212
Finally, Halflife issues a passport for itself, under the same rules.

32
00:02:49,532 --> 00:02:54,625
It comes back INVALID — 15 advisories in its own closure. We published it anyway.

33
00:02:55,433 --> 00:02:57,531
Halflife. No claim without measurement.
